<DocumentLayout title="Sources & limitations" description="The primary sources behind the redaction proposal, what they support, and what remains untested." reference="Source notes" heading="A proposal needs a record." deck="The basis for the legal and security distinctions on this site, and the limits of the environmental case.">
  <div slot="aside"><p>Sources reviewed<br />19 October 2026</p><nav aria-label="On this page"><a href="#law">01 / Legal context</a><a href="#security">02 / Secure redaction</a><a href="#evidence">03 / Evidence still needed</a></nav></div>
  <section id="law"><span class="source-type">01 / Primary source · United States federal law</span><h2>Freedom of Information Act</h2><p><a href="https://www.justice.gov/oip/freedom-information-act-5-usc-552">5 U.S.C. § 552, published by the U.S. Department of Justice</a></p><p>Section 552(b) addresses segregable material and indications of deleted information and exemptions, with exceptions where those indications would harm protected interests.</p><p><strong>Limit:</strong> this supports a specific federal-law context. It does not establish that every law, agency policy, or court rule permits every redaction appearance. This project makes no universal “no statute requires black” claim.</p></section>
  <section id="security"><span class="source-type">02 / Primary source · Court filing guidance</span><h2>Covering text is not removing it.</h2><p><a href="https://cand.uscourts.gov/cases-e-filing/cmecf-information/preparing-your-cmecf-filing">Preparing Your CM/ECF Filing — U.S. District Court, Northern District of California</a></p><p>The court warns that graphical coverings can leave underlying information recoverable, and that files may contain hidden metadata.</p><p><strong>Application here:</strong> the comparison contains only fictional public text and empty omission markers. It demonstrates appearance, not a method or tool for redacting real documents.</p></section>
  <section id="evidence"><span class="source-type">03 / Project hypothesis · Not a measured result</span><h2>The environmental case remains to be tested.</h2><p>We propose testing whether lower-coverage marks reduce ink or toner use in printed redacted records. This site publishes no measured toner savings, emissions reductions, cost savings, or agency trial results.</p><p>Printer maintenance, jam rates, processing times, staffing effects, and equipment life are separate questions. A reduction in drawn area cannot establish any of those outcomes.</p><p><a href="/about/#trial">The proposed trial</a> describes what would need to be measured. The IRES name describes this project’s proposal; it is not evidence of adoption or endorsement.</p></section>
</DocumentLayout>
